  "answer_text": "**Yes, Coca-Cola originally contained cocaine.** However, it has been completely cocaine-free for over a century. \n\nThe story of how cocaine got into\u2014and was eventually removed from\u2014one of the world's most famous beverages is rooted in 19th-century medicine and clever marketing.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why was there cocaine in Coca-Cola?\nCoca-Cola was invented in 1885 by Dr. John Stith Pemberton, an Atlanta pharmacist who had become addicted to morphine after being wounded in the American Civil War. Seeking a cure for his addiction, he formulated a sweet, carbonated patent medicine. \n\nIn the late 19th century, cocaine was completely legal, unregulated, and widely hailed by the medical community as a miracle cure-all for fatigue, headaches, depression, and addiction. \n\nThe drink\u2019s name came directly from its two key medicinal ingredients:\n*   **\"Coca\"** from the extract of Peruvian **coca leaves** (the plant source of cocaine).\n*   **\"Cola\"** from the **kola nut** (a natural source of caffeine).\n\n### 2. How much cocaine was in the original drink?\nWhile it is difficult to know the exact dosage because early recipes were kept secret, historians estimate that an average glass of Coca-Cola in the late 1880s contained roughly **9 milligrams of cocaine**. \n\nFor comparison, a modern illicit recreational dose (a \"line\" of cocaine) is typically around 50 to 75 milligrams. While 9 milligrams was not enough to cause a severe high, it was enough to provide a mild, euphoric buzz and stimulant effect.\n\n### 3. When and why was it removed?\nBy the late 1890s and early 1900s, public opinion on cocaine shifted dramatically. Society began realizing the drug's highly addictive nature, and a moral and racial panic emerged in the American South, linking cocaine use to crime.\n\n*   **1903 (The Reduction):** To protect the company's reputation, Coca-Cola president Asa Candler ordered the removal of nearly all active cocaine from the formula. The company switched from fresh coca leaves to \"spent\" or **decocainized coca leaves**.\n*   **1929 (The Complete Elimination):** Chemical extraction processes were finally perfected to remove even the tiniest trace precursors of cocaine (such as ecgonine). Since 1929, the drink has been 100% cocaine-free.\n\n### 4. Does Coca-Cola still use coca leaves today?\n**Surprisingly, yes.** Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves to flavor the soda, but they are completely stripped of their active narcotic alkaloids first. \n\nBecause of international drug laws, importing coca leaves is illegal for almost everyone, but Coca-Cola has a special, highly regulated exemption. A chemical processing facility in Maywood, New Jersey, owned by the **Stepan Company**, is the only entity in the United States authorized by the DEA to import coca leaves (primarily from Peru). \n\nAt this facility:\n1.  The coca leaves are processed to extract the cocaine-free flavoring agent, which is shipped directly to Coca-Cola to make their secret syrup.\n2.  The extracted active cocaine is sold to Mallinckrodt, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a legal prescription topical anesthetic used by doctors for ear, nose, and throat surgeries.",
